@@ -0,0 +1,62 @@
+# US-21: Contain failed jobs
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| Epic | WBMME-2 Model Training & Selection |
+| Requirement | MLE-NF-006 |
+| Points / sprint | 5 / Sprint 2 |
+| Owner | Varnika (Member 2) |
+| Related risk | SAD R3 (a bad job takes down the server) |
+
+**Story.** As a user I want a training job that fails to affect only that job, so that one bad run cannot stop the site or other people's work.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+- [x] Training runs in a **separate process** that shares nothing with the web server.
+- [x] An exception, a crash, a kill (for example out of memory) or a hang fails **only that run**.
+- [x] A hung job is stopped after the time limit (`WBMME_JOB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`, default 300 s).
+- [x] The run shows status **Failed** and a plain message; technical detail and server paths go to the server log only.
+- [x] A failed run leaves **no model file and no scratch folder** behind. A model is moved to its final place only after the job reports success.
+- [x] The next job runs normally after a failure.
+- [x] A run left "Queued" or "Running" after a server restart is shown as Failed (interrupted) when its status is read.
+
+## How it works
+
+```
+POST /api/train -> TrainingRun(queued) -> server thread -> runner.run()
+                                                         |-- starts a new Python process (spawn) --> worker.run_job()
+                                                         |       writes result.json (+ model.joblib on success) in a scratch folder
+                                                         '-- waits with a time limit, reads result.json
+success: model moved into place with one atomic rename, scratch folder deleted
+failure: scratch folder deleted, run marked Failed with a safe message
+```
+
+| File | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `training/worker.py` | Code that runs inside the job process; never raises, always leaves a result file |
+| `training/runner.py` | Starts the process, enforces the time limit, turns every outcome into a result or `JobFailed` |
+| `training/jobs.py` | Status changes, scratch folder, promote on success, stale-run check |
+| `training/artifacts.py` | `work_dir`, `promote`, `discard` |
+| `training/tests/faults.py` | Fault injection: crash, hang, raise, out of memory, partial file |
+| `training/tests/test_containment.py` | The tests, using real child processes |
+
+## Evidence
+
+| Injected fault | Result |
+| --- | --- |
+| Process dies with no result (exit 137) | Failed, "Training stopped unexpectedly..." |
+| Exception outside the worker's handler | Failed, same message |
+| Half-written model, then crash | Failed, nothing promoted |
+| `MemoryError` during training | Failed, generic message; detail only in the log |
+| Never finishes (limit set to 2 s) | Killed, Failed, "took longer than 2 seconds" |
+| Claims success without a model | Failed, generic message |
+| Crash, then a normal job | Second job completes |
+
+Full suite: **158 tests pass**, 100% statement coverage of the job, runner, worker and artifact modules. No child process is left running after the tests.
+
+## Design notes (for the viva)
+
+- **Why a process and not just a thread?** A thread shares memory with the web server: an out-of-memory error or a native-library crash in a thread can end the whole server. A separate process can die alone.
+- **Why `spawn`?** It starts a clean interpreter, works on Windows and Linux, and cannot inherit the server's database connection.
+- **Why a result file?** The child can die at any moment; a small file written atomically at the end is a simple, reliable signal. No file means the job did not finish.
+- **Limit.** There is no per-process memory cap. An administrator can add one in the operating system; the design already treats an out-of-memory kill as an ordinary contained failure.
